  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Continued dry with seasonable temperatures through \nthe middle of next week. \n\nThe latest WX analysis indicates an amplified upper level\npattern, not quite an Omega block, but a strong enough upper\nlevel ridge across the central CONUS to keep the upstream \ntroughing over the SW from getting east of the Plains states \nthrough the middle of next week. Meanwhile, light northerly flow\naloft will continue across the local area through Sunday, \nbefore weak shortwave energy passes through and moves off the SE\ncoast Sunday night. At the surface, strong high pressure (~1034\nmb) is centered over eastern NY state and New England, ridging \nsouthward into the mid-Atlantic. The pressure gradient remains \ncompressed over southern VA and the Carolinas, keeping the \nairmass mixed and milder than 24 hrs ago for most- temperatures \nare primarily in the 40s and 50s, though a few upper 30s are \nnoted over the north closer to the sfc high. Any frost should be\npatchy at best through sunrise and confined to the northern \npiedmont.\n\nThe high continues to gradually build south and into the local \narea later today and tonight, but with a sfc trough of low \npressure off the SE US coast, and somewhat lower heights\naloft/weak shortwaves aloft through Sunday, expect a lot of\nshallow low level moisture to again lead to a period of partly-\nmostly cloudy skies late this morning and afternoon despite no\nrain chances. Highs will be a few degrees cooler than yesterday,\nbut seasonable in the low-mid 60s north (and along the coast) \nand mid to upper 60s interior central and south. Breezy E-NE\nwinds near the coast will continue, while winds inland will\ngenerally be ~10 mph or less. For tonight/Sunday morning,\ntemperatures should be slightly lower with mid 30s NW and upper\n30s to lower 40s for most of central and south-central VA. Patchy\nfrost is expected for areas to the N and W of Richmond, though \nconfidence for enough coverage to warrant a Frost Advisory is \nlow and will let the dayshift make the call. \n\nAs the high continues to settle in from the north Sunday, clouds\nwill tend to be a bit less, but SCT-BKN cumulus is still likely\nalong and S of I-64. Temperatures warm a few degrees with highs\nin the mid 60s to near 70 F. Sunday night/Monday AM will likely\nsee the greatest potential for a more widespread frost spreading\neast from the piedmont to interior SE VA as better decoupling is\nexpected. Continue to go a few degrees colder than NBM, with\nmid 30s forecast for much of the piedmont and and mid-upper 30s\nfor interior SE VA. Warmer Monday ahead of the next (dry) front,\nthen slightly cooler into midweek and staying dry. The ECMWF\nensemble chance for just .01\" of rain through the end of the\nmonth is less than 10% for most of the CWA, with the GEFS only\nslightly higher. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Elevated tidal anomalies continue through Saturday \nwith nuisance to minor tidal flooding forecast.\n\nThe combination of swell still propagating toward the coast and\nelevated NE winds has kept tidal anomalies at 1.5-2ft above \nnormal. These anomalies should remain steady and/or slowly fall \ntoday. Therefore, primarily nuisance to minor tidal flooding is\nexpected and Coastal Flood Advisories remain in effect through \nthe morning/early aftn high tide cycle for some locations, \nprimarily for the tidal Rappahannock, southern shore of the \ntidal Potomac, the upper York, and the middle and upper Ches. \nBay. Nuisance flooding may persist into tonight/Sunday as swell\ncontinues to propagate toward the coast with persistent (but \nweaker) onshore flow, as well as a gradual increase in astronomical\nhigh tides as we approach full moon.",
